@

We extract the grade and then figure out which grading scale it belongs to,
so we can put it in the list that scale is averaged in.

This classification used to carry two branches of its own, one appending [[P]]
for \enquote{complete} and one appending [[F]] for \enquote{incomplete}.
They cannot run any more: [[normalize_grade]] has already turned those
spellings into [[P]] and [[F]], and the P/F branch appends the very same value
to the very same list.
Dropping them is thus behaviour-preserving for every grade Canvas reports, and
it removes the last copy of the alias vocabulary from this module---which is
the point of moving it to \cref{gradealiases}.

One case does change, and for the better.
The removed branches called [[casefold]] on the grade unguarded, so a
non-string grade---a numeric survey score, say---used to raise an
[[AttributeError]] from the middle of the classification.
Such a grade now falls through to the [[else]] branch and raises this module's
own [[ValueError]], which names the grade and the assignment it came from.
<<add grade to the correct grades list>>=
grade = normalize_grade(submission.grade)

if grade in ["A", "B", "C", "D", "E"]:
  a2e_grades.append(grade)
elif grade in ["P", "F"]:
  pf_grades.append(grade)
else:
  raise ValueError(f"Unknown grade {grade} for assignment {assignment}.")
